  <data-orientation>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{16B091ED-F449-469A-86DF-57E3343BD55B}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the average fare paid by passengers across different age groups. Using an aggregated fare measure provides a broader comparison of fare values among the different stages of the age range represented. The results show that passengers aged 31–50 had the highest average fare at $256.59, while passengers aged 51 and above had the lowest average fare at $240.27. The 1–17 age group had an average fare of $252.21, followed by passengers aged 18–30 with an average fare of $244.68.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{A2B8E095-D601-4F4B-9D8F-F7B01C7BC7E0}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the proportion of passengers who survived within each passenger class. Using a percentage rather than a simple survivor count accounts for differences in class size and provides a more comparable measure or survival across the three classes. Looking at the bar graphs, the survival proportions were relatively similar with a little more than 48% surviving from the first and second classes at 48.17% and 48.47% repectively while highest proportion of survivors came from the third class with 51.10%.  </desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{4445DB10-B1FE-4B3B-B873-0C91BD306894}' />
      <desc>This heat map examines the composition of the passenger population across passenger class and sex. Second class had the most male passengers with 188, while third class had the fewest male passengers with 157. First class had the most female passengers with 173, while second class had the fewest female passengers with 138. Overall, the heat map shows how the number of male and female passengers varied across the three passenger classes.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{BB9E959B-5398-4FCE-87CD-401D2A218A13}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the proportion of passengers who survived across different age groups. By using survival percentages rather than simply comparing passenger counts, the visualization provides a clearer comparison of survival outcomes among the different age groups represented in the dataset. The results show that passengers aged 51 and above had the lowest survival rate at 47.46%, while passengers aged 18–30 had the highest survival rate close to 54%. Passengers aged 1–17 had a survival rate slightly higher than 49%, while those aged 31–50 had a survival rate of a little over 48%.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{0A79E7A9-EED2-4107-881A-53CC02C8D134}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the proportion of passengers who survived across sex categories. Using a calculated survival percentage rather than a simple passenger count provides a more comparable view of survival outcomes between the groups represented in the dataset. Male passengers had a survival rate of 49.91%, while female passengers had a slightly lower survival rate of 48.41%.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{8D76BE1C-6C43-4B1B-8429-588EF8367592}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the proportion of passengers within each passenger class whose fare was above the overall average fare. By using the overall average as a calculated benchmark, the visualization examines how the proportion of higher-than-average fares differs across the three passenger classes. The results show that 46.48% of first-class passengers paid above the overall average fare, while second class had the highest proportion at 52.15%. Third class followed with 50.47% of passengers paying above the overall average fare.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{36085318-FD77-4D51-87C7-9E54B8B87C95}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares the average family size associated with male and female passengers. By examining the average number of people represented within each passenger&apos;s family group, the visualization highlights differences in family structure between the two sex categories. Female passengers had an average family size of exactly 5.00, while the average family size associated with male passengers was slightly higher at 5.07.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
  </data-orientation>
</workbook>
